title: Vaginal Discharge
---

# Vaginal Discharge

- **Natural defense mechanism of the female genital tract:**
  - <u>Physical barrier</u>:
    - Apposition of labia and vaginal walls
    - Natural resistance to infection by stratified squamous epithelium
  - <u>Chemical barrier</u> - vaginal acidity (pH 3.5-4.5)
  - <u>Biological barrier</u> - vaginal microbiome (esp. lactobacilli)
- **Factors adversely affecting defense mechanism:**
  - <u>Menstrual cycle</u> - alkalinity of secretion around menses
  - <u>Pregnancy and pueperium</u>:
    - Local factors - trauma at delivery reduces barrier effect of stratified squamous epithelium; increased glycogen content in vaginal secretions (due to increased estrogen levels)
    - Chemical factors - increase in pH of vaginal secretion; lochia is alkaline
- **Etiology of vaginal discharge:**
  - **Non-infectious causes:**
    - <u>Physiological</u>
    - <u>Cervical ectropian</u> - columnar epithelium of endocervix seen in exocervix
    - <u>Foreign bodies</u>
    - <u>Vulval dermatitis</u> and related skin conditions
    - <u>Neoplastic</u> - benign and malignant tumours
  - **Infectious causes:**
    - <u>Non-sexually transmitted</u> - Candidiasis, GBS, bacterial vaginosis
    - <u>Sexually transmitted</u> - Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoea, Trichomonas vaginalis
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - **HPI** - onset, progression, quality, timing:
    - <u>Onset and progression</u> - acute vs chronic
    - <u>Quality</u> - colour and odour
    - <u>Timing</u> - in relation w/ menses or previous intstrumentation
  - **Associated Sx:**
    - Abdominal pain
    - Fever
    - Pruritis
  - **Sexual and contraceptive Hx:**
    - <u>Recent instrumentation w/ IUD</u> - predisposes to gynaecological infections
  - **Previous obstetrics Hx** - recent delivery increases risk of infection
  - <u>Past health</u> - immunocomprimised, previous episodes
  - **PMH:**
    - <u>Immunocomprimised states</u> - e.g. DM, drugs, autoimmune conditions
    - Previous episodes of gynaecologial infections
  - **Cervical smear Hx**
- **P/E:**
  - Vital signs
  - Visualisation of vaginal discharge
  - Abdomino-pelvic examination
- **Approach to vaginal discharge in gynaecological patients:**
  - Differentiation between physiological and pathological discharge
  - Appropriate investigations
  - Appropriate Tx
  - Prevent recurrence
